// Mars Exploration Rovers (MER): the resource arbiter of the rovers' flight software, after Feng, Kwiatkowska and
// Parker ("Automated learning of probabilistic assumptions for compositional reasoning", FASE 2011), as used by
// 1-2-3-Go! (Azeem et al., VMCAI 2025, arXiv:2410.18293). Translated from the 1-2-3-Go! artifact's
// test-data/models/mer.jani (github.com/muqsit-azeem/dtstrat-123go-artifact, commit 805665e8), with three changes
// that leave Pmax [ F "err" ] unchanged:
// - every command is labelled, so the policy schedules every step (the artifact's action-named models do the same);
//   the unlabelled ones are named arb_reclaim, u<i>_ready, u<i>_use, u<i>_want_comm and u<i>_want_drive;
// - a label that is disabled leaves the state unchanged (each module's `!en_<label> -> true` commands), so every
//   policy action is enabled everywhere; this replaces the model's self-loops (module `loops`, and the arbiter's
//   idle commands at s = 1 and s = 2), which are dropped;
// - variables are local to the module that writes them, as in the PRISM original.
// Two users share two resources, communication (comm, 1) and driving (drive, 2). The arbiter grants, denies and
// rescinds them. The monitor (module property) reaches e = 3 when both resources are granted at once.

mdp

const int n = {{ n }};          // how many of user 1's comm requests while drive is held may take the shortcut
const double x = {{ x }};       // probability that a user keeps a resource after using it (it must then cancel)

